  THE FEATURE-10 AUDIT -- every paired contrast, re-read with voicing and
  loudness DROPPED from the features (`m3_timbre_features`, 7 groups).
  `echo` cannot distinguish a real effect from one feature being driven
  toward zero variance and amplified by the sd floor; `artic` can.
    contrast            d echo             d ARTIC
    taught - none   +0.0823 +- 0.0101  +0.1313 +- 0.0133  (8.1 SE vs 9.9 SE)
    yoked  - none   +0.0672 +- 0.0107  +0.1153 +- 0.0136  (6.3 SE vs 8.4 SE)
    taught - yoked  +0.0151 +- 0.0082  +0.0160 +- 0.0093  (1.8 SE vs 1.7 SE)

  THE CLAIM SURVIVES THE AUDIT. `yoked - none` is +0.1153 +- 0.0136
  (8.4 SE) on features that contain no voicing and no loudness, so
  reward raises the echo in the ARTICULATORS and the published
  +0.0673 is not a feature-10 artefact.

  DIFFERENTIAL SAMPLING -- 159 scored trials on the thinnest arm
  against 200 on the fullest (80%). Asleep echo trials are skipped
  rather than scored, so the arms differ in POWER and in what the
  creature was doing, even though chance stays 0.500 per pair.

  SECONDARY, paired taught - none echo   +0.0823 +- 0.0101  (8.1 SE, n=36)
  the power          at this n the smallest resolvable change is 0.0202
                     (2 SE), which is 4% of the taught arm's margin
                     over chance. A null here BOUNDS what the lesson
                     costs the echo; it does not show it costs nothing.

  THE DISCRIMINATOR -- is the taught arm's excess the naming response?
    yoked echo                   0.940+-0.007   (none 0.873, taught 0.955)
    paired taught - yoked        +0.0151 +- 0.0082 (1.8 SE, n=36)
    yoked naming over its null   -0.0027    (taught +0.1790)
    its scored trials            162 of 200 (taught 159, none 190)

  WHAT LIVING A SESSION COSTS, paired none - fresh   -0.0156 +- 0.0070 (2.2 SE)

  PRIMARY, both on the SAME 36 creatures
    (a) echo on the taught arm   0.955  against the 0.75 bar      MET
    (b) naming above its null    +0.1790 +- 0.0197 (9.1 SE)   MET

  MET -- ONE CREATURE DOES BOTH. The same individual, in the same
  session: taught to name four words conditionally on an index it
  derives from its own ear (+0.1790 over its own null, 9.1 SE), and
  still echoing which of those four words it just heard at 0.955 from
  the voice alone, against a 0.75 bar and with the ear down to 0.574.
  So teaching four-word naming does NOT cost the echo.

  WHAT THIS DOES NOT SETTLE, and the first run of it overstated:
  the scored window is when a taught creature ANSWERS, so the taught
  arm's excess over `none` may be the naming response rather than a
  second capability. Read THE DISCRIMINATOR above before calling this
  composition: the base echo is independent of the lesson (`fresh` has
  it untaught), the EXCESS is what is in question.
